5. Nc3 ( 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Nf3 ( 6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Nf3 { The same position as 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.Nc3. } ) ( 6. Bc4 e6 { Supports the knight on d5, so the bishop on c4 bites on granite. } ) 6... Nc6 ) ( 5. Nf3 e6 6. Nc3 { The same position as the main line. } ) ( 5. c5 b6 { Hits the c5 pawn before White can support it with b4. If cxb6, Black takes back with the queen, which then hits d4 and b2. } ) 5... e6 { Supports d5 and opens the bishop. Black does not mind a trade on d5, because it leaves White with an isolated pawn on d4. } 6. Nf3 ( 6. Bg5 Be7 7. Nf3 O-O ) ( 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nf3 Be7 { The same position as the main line after 7.cxd5 Nxd5. } ) ( 6. c5 Be7 { Develops and gets ready to castle. Later ...b6 or ...e5 can break up White's queenside pawns. } ) 6... Be7 { Gets ready to castle, and keeps the choice of how to recapture on d5. } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 { Recaptures with the knight, which now stands on a strong central square. White has an isolated pawn on d4. } 8. Bd3 O-O { King safe. Black can now aim at d4, the pawn with no neighbours to protect it. } 9. O-O Nc6 { Adds a second attacker to d4. Black's plan: blockade the d4 pawn with a knight on d5, trade pieces and aim for a good endgame. If White varies, keep a knight on d5 and trade pieces. } *
